
# ── Journey 8: Influence & Persuasion ───────────────────────────────────────
levels.append({'id':71,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Understanding Your Audience',
'scenario':'You need to persuade three different people of the same thing: that your team\'s project should receive additional budget. Each person has different priorities and communication styles. Your director cares about strategic alignment. Your CFO cares about financial return. A sceptical colleague on the approval panel cares about execution risk. You need to tailor your case to each.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Your director is strategically focused on the company\'s five-year growth plan. How do you frame the budget request for her?',
'"This project directly advances Pillar Two of our five-year plan — the capability we\'re building does not exist elsewhere in the organisation and it positions us for the market expansion we\'ve committed to."',
['"This project will save us money and time in the short term."','"This project has a strong ROI and the team is ready to execute immediately."','Frame it as a quick win: "We can show results within three months."'],
'Leading with strategic alignment — the specific pillar and the specific capability gap — is exactly what a strategically focused director needs to hear first.',
'Short-term savings is a CFO frame, not a strategy frame. ROI is also more relevant to the CFO. A quick win frame may actually work against you with a director focused on a five-year plan.'),
mcq(2,'You now speak to the CFO. He is focused on financial return and risk-adjusted value. How do you frame the same request?',
'"The base case shows a 2.4x return on investment over three years. The conservative case is 1.6x. I\'ve stress-tested the assumptions and can walk you through each one if helpful."',
['"This project will really strengthen our competitive position in the market."','"The team believes strongly in this and morale will benefit from moving forward."','Frame it as a team development opportunity: "We\'ll build capability we\'ve been lacking."'],
'A CFO needs specific numbers: the return multiple, the timeframe, and the reassurance that the assumptions have been tested.',
'Competitive position is a strategic frame, not a financial frame. Team morale is irrelevant to a CFO\'s approval decision. Capability development is useful but secondary to the financial return.'),
mcq(3,'Your sceptical colleague on the panel focuses on execution risk. How do you frame your case for him?',
'"The three highest execution risks are: [name them]. We have a specific mitigation for each: [name them]. The team has done this successfully at a smaller scale and I\'m managing personally."',
['"The financial case is strong and the director has already indicated support."','Tell him the project is low risk and the team is fully committed.','Focus on the upside: "If this works, the impact will be transformative."'],
'A risk-focused audience needs you to name the risks before they do — and immediately pair each with a specific mitigation. Your personal accountability adds credibility.',
'Citing the director\'s support is social proof, not risk analysis. "Low risk and committed team" is an assertion, not a risk mitigation. Focusing on upside with a risk-focused person signals that you are not taking their concern seriously.'),
mcq(4,'In the panel meeting, the CFO and your sceptical colleague are both present. You need to speak to both in the same moment. How do you do it?',
'"I want to speak to both the financial return and the execution risk, since I know those are the two critical questions here."',
['Default to the financial frame since the CFO is the most powerful person in the room.','Use the strategic frame since it is the most defensible.','Wait for them to ask the questions and then respond to each separately.'],
'Explicitly naming both priorities and committing to address each is the most inclusive and efficient approach in a mixed-audience room.',
'Defaulting to the CFO\'s frame makes the sceptical colleague feel their concern is not being addressed. The strategic frame speaks to neither of the two concerns present. Waiting for them to ask gives you no control over the order or emphasis.'),
mcq(5,'After the presentation, the sceptical colleague says, "I have one more question about execution — if the key person leaves mid-project, what happens?" How do you answer?',
'"We have a documented handover plan and a named backup within the team for every critical role. That documentation exists today — it\'s not just a plan, it\'s already done."',
['"I\'m confident that won\'t happen — the team is fully committed."','Say, "That\'s a fair concern — I\'ll look at it before the decision."','Say, "We\'d cross that bridge if we came to it.'],
'Providing a specific, pre-existing mitigation — the documentation already exists — is the most compelling answer to an execution risk question. It converts "we would" into "we have."',
'"That won\'t happen" dismisses a legitimate risk scenario with confidence that is not evidence. "I\'ll look at it before the decision" signals the concern was not anticipated. "Cross that bridge if we come to it" is the worst possible answer to a risk-focused questioner.')],
'response':resp('You tailored your argument to three different audience priorities — strategic, financial, risk — addressed both financial and execution concerns in a mixed audience, and answered a specific execution risk question with a specific pre-existing mitigation. Now type or say aloud the one-page persuasion brief you would write for someone who cares about all three of these priorities simultaneously.',
'Excellent! Understanding your audience is the first and most important act of persuasion. Before you say a word about your idea, you must know who you are speaking to and what they most need to hear. You showed that today.')})

levels.append({'id':72,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'The Art of Framing',
'scenario':'You want to persuade your team to adopt a new, more structured approach to their weekly work planning. Some team members resist structured processes and will immediately push back if you present it as "a new process." You need to frame it in a way that makes adoption more likely — not by deceiving them but by finding the framing that genuinely reflects the value it creates.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You are introducing the new planning approach. Which framing makes adoption most likely without being manipulative?',
'"I want to talk about something that I think will give us all more headspace during the week — a simple structure for planning that protects focus time. I want to show you what I mean."',
['"I\'m introducing a new process — it\'s mandatory from next week."','Frame it as "everyone else is doing it" to create social pressure.','Tell the team it will save them time, even if you are not sure it will.'],
'Framing around the benefit they will feel — more headspace, protected focus time — rather than the structure you are imposing makes the proposal about them, not about the system.',
'"Mandatory from next week" is the framing most likely to generate resistance. Social pressure framing is manipulative and often backfires with independent thinkers. Claiming time savings you are not sure about sets up a credibility risk.'),
mcq(2,'A team member says, "This sounds like more overhead." How do you reframe?',
'"I understand it looks like overhead. What I\'m trying to do is shift some of the mental load from during the week to fifteen minutes on Monday. Less overhead overall — just front-loaded."',
['"It\'s not overhead — it\'s a structured approach to managing complexity."','Say, "I know, but it\'s something we need to do as a team."','Dismiss the concern: "Give it four weeks — you\'ll see it\'s worth it."'],
'Acknowledging the perception — "it looks like overhead" — and reframing it as a trade: fifteen focused minutes on Monday for less dispersed mental load during the week is honest and specific.',
'"It\'s not overhead" directly contradicts their characterisation without explaining why. "We need to do it as a team" is a compliance frame. "Give it four weeks" is a deferral of the concern rather than an engagement with it.'),
mcq(3,'Another team member says, "I already have my own system that works for me." How do you respond?',
'"That\'s great — I don\'t want to replace what\'s working for you. I\'d like us to have one shared touchpoint per week so the team has collective visibility, and within that you can keep your own system."',
['Tell them the team system supersedes individual systems.','Say, "I\'m curious — what does your system look like? Maybe we can incorporate it."','Concede entirely: "Fair enough — if your system works, you don\'t have to change it."'],
'Separating the individual system from the collective touchpoint — these are different and compatible — removes the threat and reframes the ask as additive rather than replacive.',
'"The team system supersedes individual systems" is the compliance frame in more explicit terms. Asking about their system is curious and collaborative but may delay the main point. Fully conceding means the shared touchpoint does not happen.'),
mcq(4,'The team asks, "Can we trial it for three weeks before committing?" How do you respond to this request?',
'"Yes — a three-week trial is exactly right. I\'d ask that we all genuinely commit to the full structure for three weeks so we can assess it fairly. Let\'s agree what fair assessment looks like at the end."',
['Agree to the trial but reduce the structure so it feels less burdensome.','Say, "I\'d prefer we just commit — a trial suggests we\'re not sure it\'s right."','Agree to the trial and say nothing about what success looks like, to avoid pressure.'],
'Agreeing to the trial, insisting on full commitment during it, and defining success criteria upfront are the three conditions that make a trial actually useful rather than a polite delay.',
'Reducing the structure during the trial means the team is not testing the actual thing. "I\'d prefer we just commit" forecloses the collaborative offer the team just made. Agreeing without defining success criteria means the end of the trial produces no clear answer.'),
mcq(5,'After three weeks, the team reports it is working. One initially resistant member says, "I thought I\'d hate this but it\'s actually helped me prioritise." How do you close?',
'"I\'m really glad — and I want to say, the fact that you gave it a genuine try is what made it possible to find that out. That\'s not nothing."',
['Say, "I knew you\'d come around — this approach is proven."','Take credit: "Sometimes a bit of structure is what a team needs — you\'re welcome."','Say, "Great — let\'s move to bi-weekly planning now that we have the rhythm."'],
'Attributing the positive result to their genuine commitment — which is also true — is gracious and accurate. It reinforces the behaviour you want to continue.',
'"I knew you\'d come around" is a slightly patronising I-told-you-so. Taking credit removes their agency from the outcome. Immediately escalating to bi-weekly planning is premature and could feel like you are pushing again.')],
'response':resp('You framed the proposal around the benefit rather than the structure, reframed overhead as front-loaded mental load, separated individual and collective systems to remove the threat, agreed to a trial with clear conditions, and closed by attributing the result to the team\'s commitment. Now type or say aloud the one-paragraph "deal memo" you would write at the end of the three-week trial to capture what worked and what you would adjust.',
'Brilliant! Framing is not spin — it is the work of finding the angle from which your idea is most clearly and honestly seen. When the frame is true and the audience recognises themselves in it, resistance dissolves. You found that angle today.')})

levels.append({'id':73,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Building a Compelling Case',
'scenario':'You want to convince your organisation\'s leadership to invest in a mental health support programme for staff. You believe deeply in this cause but you know that emotional arguments alone will not move this audience. You need to build a compelling, multi-layered case that speaks to both the human and the business dimensions.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You open your presentation to leadership. How do you establish the problem?',
'"One in four of our employees report experiencing significant workplace stress. Our turnover rate has risen 18% in the past two years. These numbers are connected — and they represent a real cost to this organisation and to real people."',
['"Mental health is an important issue that I think we all agree on."','Open with a personal story about your own mental health struggles to build empathy.','Start with the solution and explain the problem as context.'],
'Connecting two specific data points — employee stress and rising turnover — and naming both the human and financial dimension establishes the problem with both credibility and relevance.',
'"An important issue we all agree on" is consensus-building without data. A personal story can be powerful but may shift the framing from a business case to a personal advocacy. Leading with the solution before the problem removes the persuasive structure that makes the solution feel necessary.'),
mcq(2,'A board member asks, "What is the financial cost of this to the business?" How do you respond?',
'"Conservative estimate: each percentage point of turnover above our baseline costs us approximately X in rehiring and lost productivity. Our current excess turnover is 18 points — that is an annual cost of approximately Y. The programme costs Z."',
['Say, "The financial cost is hard to quantify but the human cost is clear."','Present a range so wide it provides no real information: "Somewhere between one million and fifty million."','Say, "I can have someone from finance get back to you on that."'],
'Specific, connected numbers — turnover cost per point, current excess points, programme cost — give the board member exactly the comparison they need to make a decision.',
'"Hard to quantify" is exactly what a board member will hear as "I have not done the financial work." A range that wide is less useful than no number at all. Deferring to finance signals you do not own the financial case.'),
mcq(3,'A sceptical leader asks, "Is there evidence that programmes like this actually improve outcomes?" How do you handle the evidence?',
'"Yes — and here is what it shows: organisations with structured mental health support see an average 23% reduction in stress-related absences and a measurable improvement in retention within eighteen months. I can share the research references."',
['Speak to your own belief in the programme rather than citing external evidence.','Share one anecdote about a company that tried something similar.','Say, "The evidence is still emerging but the intuition is strong."'],
'Citing specific, external evidence — percentage reduction, timeframe, specific outcome — and offering to share references is the most credible response to a sceptical, evidence-seeking leader.',
'Personal belief is not evidence and will not move a sceptical leader. A single anecdote is interesting but not statistically meaningful. "The intuition is strong" is the phrase that loses a sceptic entirely.'),
mcq(4,'Another leader raises a concern: "What if people see this as a sign that the company is acknowledging there is a problem — could it create liability?" How do you address this?',
'"That\'s a legitimate legal consideration. Based on what I understand, proactive investment in employee wellbeing actually reduces liability exposure compared to inaction. I would recommend we get legal\'s view on the specific implementation before we launch — I\'ve already flagged this with them."',
['Dismiss the concern: "I don\'t think that\'s a real risk — other companies do this openly."','Admit you have not thought about this and offer to look into it.','Redirect: "The reputational benefit of offering this programme outweighs the risk."'],
'Acknowledging the legitimacy of the concern, offering your understanding of the direction, and naming that you have already flagged it with legal shows preparation and appropriate risk-awareness.',
'"I don\'t think that\'s a real risk" dismisses a concern that your counsel may have a specific view on. Admitting you have not thought about it undermines your preparation. Redirecting without addressing the specific concern can look evasive to a legal mind.'),
mcq(5,'The chair says, "I\'m persuaded by the case — but I want to see a pilot before a full commitment." How do you respond?',
'"A pilot is exactly right. I\'d propose a ninety-day pilot with one hundred employees, with specific measurement criteria we agree on today — retention signal, absence rate, and employee-reported wellbeing. I can have a proposal to you by Friday."',
['Agree but without proposing specific parameters, to avoid over-committing.','Push for full commitment: "A pilot will take too long — employees need this now."','Agree and say you will define the pilot criteria yourself without further input.'],
'Embracing the pilot, proposing specific scope, naming specific measurement criteria, and committing to a delivery date turns the conditional approval into a forward-moving next step.',
'Agreeing without parameters leaves the pilot undefined. Pushing back against the pilot when the chair has offered it is an unwise use of the political capital you just built. Defining the criteria yourself without joint input removes the accountability that makes the chair\'s conditional approval meaningful.')],
'response':resp('You established the problem with connected data points, answered the financial question with specific numbers, responded to a sceptic with external evidence, addressed a legal concern with preparation and appropriate referral, and embraced the pilot with specific, measurable criteria. Now type or say aloud the pilot proposal you would send the chair by Friday.',
'Outstanding! Building a compelling case requires you to anticipate your audience\'s resistance and address it before it is raised. You did that — with data, evidence, legal awareness, and a specific pilot proposal. That is professional persuasion at its best.')})

levels.append({'id':74,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Negotiating for What You Want',
'scenario':'You are negotiating your salary for a new role you have been offered. The offer is below what you believe your market value and your experience warrant. You have done your research, you know your number, and you want to negotiate professionally — without being aggressive or damaging the relationship before you have even started.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'The hiring manager has just given you the offer. It is below what you were expecting. What is your immediate response?',
'"Thank you — I\'m genuinely excited about this role and this team. I\'d like to take a day to review the full package before I respond. Is that okay?"',
['Accept immediately to show enthusiasm and avoid any awkwardness.','Say, "I was hoping for more — can you do better?" in the same conversation.','Ask immediately what the salary cap is for this role.'],
'Thanking them, expressing genuine enthusiasm, and requesting time to review the full package before responding is professional and gives you space to prepare your negotiation.',
'Accepting immediately without negotiating is the most common and costly mistake in salary discussions. "Can you do better?" in the same call is possible but reactive and unprepared. Asking the salary cap reveals your floor rather than establishing your value.'),
mcq(2,'You call back the next day. How do you open the negotiation?',
'"I\'ve had a chance to review the offer — and I want to confirm again how excited I am about this role. I\'d like to talk about the base salary, if that\'s okay. I was hoping we could get to X."',
['Open with all the things you need — salary, bonus, equity, benefits — simultaneously.','Say, "I received a competing offer for more — I\'d like you to match it."','Apologise for bringing it up: "I\'m sorry to do this but I wanted to ask about the salary."'],
'Affirming your enthusiasm, making a single clear ask — the specific number — and framing it as a conversation is the confident, respectful negotiation opening.',
'Raising everything at once can feel like an ambush and makes each item harder to address. Citing a competing offer you may not have is a gamble — if asked to confirm, you may be caught. Apologising for negotiating devalues your request before you have made it.'),
mcq(3,'The hiring manager says, "That is above our band for this level." How do you respond?',
'"I understand that there may be band constraints. Could you tell me whether there\'s any flexibility in the band, or whether there are other elements of the package that could be adjusted to get closer to that number?"',
['Back down immediately: "Okay — the original offer is fine then."','Repeat your number more firmly: "I really do need X — it\'s important to me."','Say, "That\'s disappointing to hear — let me think about whether this is the right fit."'],
'Acknowledging the band constraint while asking two specific, practical questions — band flexibility, alternative package elements — keeps the negotiation moving without backing down or escalating.',
'Immediately backing down leaves the offer below your value and signals that your negotiation was a test rather than a genuine position. Repeating the number more firmly without engaging the band constraint is slightly aggressive. "Let me think about fit" is an implicit threat that may damage the relationship.'),
mcq(4,'The manager says she can offer an additional five days of annual leave and an earlier performance review at six months. How do you evaluate this?',
'"The additional leave is meaningful and I appreciate that. The six-month review — what is a realistic salary adjustment range at that review if I perform well? That helps me understand the full picture."',
['Accept immediately — extra leave and an early review is a good result.','Decline the non-salary elements: "I only care about the base."','Ask for more time to think about it without asking any clarifying questions.'],
'Acknowledging the offer warmly, then asking the specific question that determines whether the six-month review is meaningful — what could the salary adjustment be — is smart and fair.',
'Accepting without clarifying what the early review might produce means you may be accepting less than the full value on offer. "I only care about base" is unnecessarily rigid. Asking for more time without engaging the specifics delays the process without gaining information.'),
mcq(5,'The manager confirms a potential 10-15% salary adjustment at six months if your performance meets the agreed targets. How do you close?',
'"That changes my calculus. If we can agree on the performance targets in writing before I start, I\'m happy to accept the offer as structured — with the leave and the six-month review committed."',
['Agree verbally but do not request the commitment in writing.','Ask for a higher potential adjustment at six months to push the negotiation further.','Say, "I\'ll think about it and get back to you" without making a decision.'],
'Conditional acceptance with a specific written commitment request is the ideal close — you get the deal, the milestone, and the protection of documented expectations.',
'Agreeing without the written commitment means the review targets and range may not be enforceable. Pushing for a higher adjustment percentage when you have a clear offer on the table is a risk you do not need to take. "I\'ll think about it" after a satisfactory resolution is an unnecessary delay.')],
'response':resp('You bought time professionally, opened with a single clear number, engaged with band constraints practically, asked the clarifying question that unlocked the real value of the six-month review, and closed with conditional acceptance including a written commitment request. Now type or say aloud the email you would send to confirm the agreed terms before your start date.',
'Superb! Negotiating for what you want is not aggression — it is self-advocacy. You showed that it is possible to be enthusiastic about a role and clear about your value at the same time. Those things are not in tension.')})

levels.append({'id':75,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Influencing Without Authority',
'scenario':'You are a mid-level professional who wants to change a process that affects your whole department but you have no formal authority to mandate the change. The people who need to change their behaviour are peers and some of them are slightly senior to you. You need to use influence rather than authority to move this.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'How do you approach this situation before talking to anyone?',
'Identify two or three colleagues who are respected, are already sympathetic to the change, and whose endorsement will carry weight with others.',
['Draft a formal change request document and circulate it to the whole department.','Raise it directly in the next all-hands meeting to get maximum visibility.','Begin implementing the change yourself and hope others follow.'],
'Starting with respected, sympathetic allies — before making a public move — is the foundational influence strategy when you have no authority.',
'A formal document circulated to the whole department without pre-work can generate premature resistance. Raising it at all-hands without groundwork can feel like ambushing people. Implementing unilaterally may work temporarily but does not achieve the department-wide change you need.'),
mcq(2,'You speak to one of these respected colleagues and she is supportive. How do you ask for her help?',
'"Would you be willing to co-propose this with me? I think your credibility in the team gives this more weight than if I push it alone — and I genuinely believe you\'d get something from the process change too."',
['Ask her to make the proposal herself without naming your involvement.','Say, "I need you to back me up on this in the meeting."','Just tell her about it and ask what she thinks, without making a specific ask.'],
'A specific, honest ask — co-propose it, because your credibility adds weight, and here\'s what you get out of it too — is clear, fair, and respects her agency.',
'Asking her to make the proposal without your name is credit-avoidance that she may find odd. "Back me up in the meeting" is a reactive ask that gives her no time to think. Just telling her without a specific ask leaves the outcome to chance.'),
mcq(3,'You now have two respected allies and a plan. How do you introduce the proposal to the wider group?',
'"A few of us have been thinking about how we handle [process] and we\'d like to share a simple change that we think would help everyone. Can we take ten minutes at Thursday\'s team meeting?"',
['Send a department-wide email laying out the change and asking for feedback.','Ask your manager to introduce it for you to give it more authority.','Raise it informally at lunch with anyone who will listen.'],
'Framing it as "a few of us" rather than "I" signals coalition rather than personal agenda. Requesting a specific, bounded time slot is professional and respectful.',
'An email to the whole department without a conversation first gives the critics the advantage of a written record to push back on. Asking your manager to introduce it for you loses the ownership and the peer influence dynamic. Lunch conversations are useful groundwork but not a substitute for a formal introduction.'),
mcq(4,'In the team meeting, a senior peer pushes back: "This sounds like extra work for me." How do you respond without sounding defensive?',
'"I hear that. If you\'re open to a fifteen-minute walk-through of how it changes your specific workflow, I think you\'ll find it\'s less work, not more. Can we do that one-to-one this week?"',
['"It\'s not extra work — it actually saves time overall for the team."','Ask the room whether others also think it is more work.','Concede: "If it adds too much work, we can look at making it optional."'],
'Hearing the concern, offering to address it specifically in a one-to-one where you can focus on their workflow, and committing to the conversation is both empathic and strategic.',
'"It\'s not extra work" is a direct contradiction that puts him on the defensive. Asking the room whether others agree makes it a vote and gives critics an opening. Making it optional undermines the department-wide change you are trying to effect.'),
mcq(5,'The proposal is adopted by the majority of the team but two people are still resistant and not following the new process. How do you address this without escalating to management?',
'Have a brief, direct private conversation with each: "I noticed you\'re still using the old process. I\'d like to understand what\'s getting in the way — is there something about the new approach that isn\'t working for you?"',
['Ignore them and hope peer pressure eventually brings them around.','Send a team message reinforcing the process change without naming anyone.','Escalate to your manager so they can mandate it.'],
'A direct, curious, private conversation — why is this not working for you — is the most respectful and effective move before escalation.',
'Ignoring the holdouts signals that the change is optional. A group message without naming names is less direct than a private conversation. Escalating to management when you have not yet spoken to the individuals directly skips the most basic influencer move.')],
'response':resp('You built a coalition before going public, made a specific ask of your first ally, introduced the proposal as a coalition initiative, addressed a senior peer\'s concern with a one-to-one offer, and addressed holdouts directly and curiously before escalating. Now type or say aloud the one-page process change document you would circulate to confirm the agreed change.',
'Excellent! Influencing without authority is one of the most sophisticated leadership skills there is. It requires you to build relationships, understand what people care about, and find the path of least resistance to a good outcome. You did all of that today.')})

levels.append({'id':76,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Storytelling to Persuade',
'scenario':'You need to persuade your leadership team to invest in a customer experience improvement programme. The data is compelling but you have seen data alone fail to move this group before. You decide to build your case around a story — a real customer journey — that makes the data vivid and real before you present the numbers.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You decide to open with a customer story rather than data. How do you choose the story?',
'Choose a real, recent customer journey that is representative of a pattern you have identified — one that the leadership team would recognise as typical, not exceptional.',
['Choose the most dramatic negative story you have, to shock the audience into action.','Invent a composite customer story that combines elements of real cases.','Use a story from a competitor\'s customer to show what could happen here.'],
'A real, representative story that leadership recognises as typical is the most persuasive because it cannot be dismissed as an edge case.',
'The most dramatic story is memorable but can be dismissed as extreme. A composite story, if discovered, destroys your credibility. A competitor story removes the immediate personal stakes.'),
mcq(2,'You tell the story of a customer, Sarah, who tried to resolve a billing issue and was transferred five times over three weeks before giving up. How do you structure the story for maximum impact?',
'Take the audience through Sarah\'s experience step by step — each transfer, each wait, each repeated explanation — letting the frustration build naturally before naming the outcome: she cancelled.',
['Summarise the story briefly and move quickly to the data.','Tell the story from the company\'s perspective to show how hard the team was trying.','Tell Sarah\'s story and then immediately pivot to the solution.'],
'Pacing the story to let the audience feel each step — the repeated explanations, the waiting — creates emotional alignment before you make your case. The outcome "she cancelled" lands with full weight.',
'Summarising briefly removes the emotional texture that makes the story persuasive. The company perspective distances the audience from the customer experience. Pivoting immediately to the solution skips the emotional landing.'),
mcq(3,'You have told Sarah\'s story. A leader says, "That\'s a single case — how do we know it\'s representative?" How do you respond?',
'"Sarah is not unique — in our last twelve months, 34% of customers who contacted support about billing left within sixty days. Sarah is the face of a pattern, not an exception."',
['Say, "I have many more stories like this if you\'d like to hear them."','Acknowledge: "You\'re right — it may be an isolated case, but it\'s worth noting."','Say, "I chose this story because it represents the worst case — but it illustrates the principle."'],
'Connecting the story directly to a statistic — 34% churn within sixty days — turns an anecdote into a representative data point and does so at exactly the right moment.',
'Offering more stories does not resolve the "is it representative?" question with data. Acknowledging it may be isolated concedes the ground you just built. Admitting it was the worst case confirms the criticism rather than addressing it.'),
mcq(4,'You present the data. A leader says, "What is the expected impact of the programme on churn?" How do you answer?',
'"Based on benchmarks from three comparable organisations that ran similar programmes, they reduced service-related churn by an average of 40% within twelve months. Our model projects a 30% reduction to be conservative — that represents X customers retained per year."',
['Say, "It\'s hard to predict exactly — but the direction is clear."','Project a high impact to make the case more compelling.','Say, "I don\'t have a precise number — but the story makes the case for itself."'],
'Specific benchmarks from comparable organisations, a conservative projection, and the quantified outcome — X customers retained — gives leadership the number they need to make a decision.',
'"Hard to predict but the direction is clear" is the answer that ends the persuasion. Projecting high to be compelling sets up a credibility problem when results come in. "The story makes the case for itself" after being directly asked for impact data is an evasion.'),
mcq(5,'You close your case. How do you connect the story and the data into a final call to action?',
'"We started with Sarah. Sarah is 34% of our billing support contacts. The cost of not acting is X customers per year. The cost of acting is Y. Sarah\'s story is a system problem — and this programme fixes the system."',
['Close with an emotional appeal to do right by customers like Sarah.','List all the programme components as your closing.','Say, "I hope this has made the case — I\'d welcome any questions."'],
'Returning to Sarah at the close and connecting her experience explicitly to the system and the numbers creates a satisfying, complete narrative arc that unites the emotional and rational case.',
'A purely emotional close without connecting back to the data can be dismissed after the fact. Listing programme components as a close is an informational end to an emotional argument — it deflates the momentum. "I hope this has made the case" is a passive, uncertain close.')],
'response':resp('You chose a representative real story, paced it for emotional build, connected it to a statistic when challenged, gave benchmarked impact projections, and returned to Sarah at the close to unite the emotional and rational case. Now type or say aloud the one-minute version of this case that you would give in an elevator to a leader who had not heard the full presentation.',
'Remarkable! Storytelling for persuasion is not manipulation — it is the work of making abstract data feel real. Sarah made the numbers mean something. You made Sarah mean something. That is the art.')})

levels.append({'id':77,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Persuasion Ethics',
'scenario':'You are in a sales conversation with a potential client. The client is leaning toward your product but has a specific concern: she is worried it is more complex than her team can handle. You know the product has a significant learning curve but your team does offer onboarding support. You are tempted to oversell how easy it is. You decide not to.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'The client says, "Is this difficult to learn?" How do you respond honestly without losing the deal?',
'"There is a learning curve — I\'d be misleading you if I said otherwise. The good news is that we include a four-week structured onboarding with a dedicated specialist, and most teams are self-sufficient within six weeks."',
['Say, "It\'s really intuitive — most people pick it up within a few days."','Say, "It depends on the team — some find it easier than others."','Say, "It\'s complex but you\'ll figure it out — everyone does."'],
'Acknowledging the learning curve honestly, then naming the specific support structure and realistic timeline, is the foundation of an ethical sale. The client can make a real decision.',
'"Really intuitive — a few days" is an oversell that will become apparent in the implementation. "It depends on the team" is evasive and places the uncertainty on her team. "You\'ll figure it out — everyone does" is breezy and does not address the concern.'),
mcq(2,'The client says, "Our team is not very technical — I\'m worried about adoption." How do you address this without hiding a real risk?',
'"That\'s a real consideration. We\'ve had success with non-technical teams when two conditions are met: a committed internal champion and the structured onboarding I mentioned. Who in your team might play that champion role?"',
['"Non-technical teams love it — it\'s very user-friendly once you get past the initial setup."','Say, "I can\'t promise it\'ll work for your team — it depends on a lot of factors."','Promise custom support beyond what your organisation typically offers.'],
'Naming the two real conditions for success with non-technical teams and immediately asking who could play the champion role is honest risk-sharing and forward-moving.',
'"User-friendly once you get past the initial setup" is still an oversell that obscures the challenge. "I can\'t promise it\'ll work" is truthful but unhelpfully pessimistic and closes the deal. Promising custom support you cannot deliver creates a different ethical problem.'),
mcq(3,'The client asks, "Can you give me a reference from a non-technical team who succeeded with this?" You have one — but the team had a very experienced champion who was unusually technical for a "non-technical" team. How do you handle this?',
'"Yes — I\'ll send you a reference. I want to be transparent: the team had a strong internal champion who, while not in a technical role, was particularly comfortable with new systems. That context might matter to you."',
['Provide the reference without any caveats and let the client form their own view.','Say, "I don\'t have a perfect reference for this — let me look into it."','Describe the reference team as purely non-technical without mentioning the champion\'s relative technical comfort.'],
'Providing the reference with honest context about the champion\'s atypical technical comfort is the only ethical option. The client can factor this in.',
'Providing without caveat allows the client to draw false equivalence with her own team. "Let me look into it" defers when you have a relevant reference and only need to provide context. Omitting the relevant detail is a deception of omission.'),
mcq(4,'The client says, "I want to be honest with you — I\'ve had a product sold to me that didn\'t match the pitch before and it was a nightmare. I need to trust you." How do you respond?',
'"I appreciate you telling me that — and I think it\'s a fair standard to hold me to. I\'ve tried to be straight with you today, including on the learning curve and the reference caveat. If anything I\'ve said doesn\'t match your experience in the first sixty days, I want you to call me directly."',
['"I completely understand — I\'m nothing like that. Our product is exactly as advertised."','"I\'m sorry that happened to you — this industry has a lot of bad actors."','Give her an extended monologue about your company\'s values and reputation.'],
'Accepting the standard, pointing back to the specific moments of honesty in this conversation — the learning curve acknowledgment, the reference caveat — and offering a direct line creates accountability that backs up your words.',
'"I\'m nothing like that — our product is exactly as advertised" is a claim, not evidence. Commenting on bad actors in the industry is true but deflects onto others. A values monologue is exactly what someone who had been misled before would be most sceptical of.'),
mcq(5,'The client says, "I appreciate your honesty — I\'m going to proceed." How do you close?',
'"Thank you. Before we sign, I want to confirm: you understand the six-week onboarding timeline and the importance of naming an internal champion before we start. I want you to go in with clear eyes."',
['Just close the deal and move to paperwork immediately.','Congratulate yourself: "I\'m glad you could tell I was being straight with you."','Say, "Great — I\'ll send everything over and we can get going as soon as possible."'],
'Restating the two key conditions before signing is the final ethical act — it confirms she is making an informed decision, not just a decision made in the warmth of a positive conversation.',
'Moving straight to paperwork without restating the conditions allows the memory of the caveats to fade. Congratulating yourself for being honest is awkward and self-congratulatory. "We can get going as soon as possible" creates urgency that runs counter to the careful, considered tone of the whole conversation.')],
'response':resp('You acknowledged the learning curve honestly, named the real conditions for success with non-technical teams, provided a reference with transparent context, accepted the client\'s trust standard by pointing to specific moments of honesty, and confirmed the conditions once more before closing. Now type or say aloud the implementation kickoff email you would send on day one that reinforces every commitment you made in this conversation.',
'Outstanding! Persuasion ethics is not a constraint on effective selling — it is the foundation of it. The trust you built today by being straight about the learning curve and the reference is worth more than any oversell. That is what client relationships are built on.')})

levels.append({'id':78,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Handling Objections',
'scenario':'You are presenting a new initiative internally and you have encountered several objections from colleagues. Rather than deflecting or dismissing them, you decide to use each objection as an opportunity to strengthen your case and build genuine buy-in.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'A colleague objects: "We tried something like this two years ago and it failed." How do you respond?',
'"I\'m glad you brought that up — can you tell me specifically what failed? I want to make sure we\'ve learned from it and that this proposal doesn\'t repeat those mistakes."',
['Say, "That was different — this is a much better approach."','Dismiss it: "The context was different then — the comparison isn\'t really valid."','Acknowledge it and move on: "Yes, that\'s a fair point — but I think this time is different."'],
'Asking for the specific failure before defending this proposal signals that you take the history seriously and are willing to be corrected if the parallel is real.',
'"That was different — this is much better" is asserted without evidence. "The context was different" may be true but is dismissive if you have not explained how. "This time is different" acknowledges without engaging the specific failure.'),
mcq(2,'The colleague explains that the previous initiative failed because it was under-resourced. Your proposal addresses this. How do you respond?',
'"That\'s exactly the lesson I built into this proposal. The number one thing that went wrong was under-resourcing — which is why I\'ve costed this at 30% more than that initiative and included a dedicated project manager. I want to show you that on the plan."',
['Say, "I thought that might be the issue — that\'s why ours is better."','Simply thank them and move on.','Say, "We can work that out as we go — I don\'t want to over-specify at this stage."'],
'Connecting the specific historical failure to a specific structural decision in your proposal — 30% more, dedicated PM — turns an objection into evidence of your thoroughness.',
'"I thought that might be the issue" is slightly patronising and does not name the specific response. Simply thanking them and moving on loses the moment. "We can work that out as we go" signals that you have not actually learned from the past failure.'),
mcq(3,'Another colleague objects: "The timing is wrong — we\'re already stretched." How do you respond?',
'"I\'d rather hear that now than after we start. Can you tell me specifically which commitments this would conflict with? I want to understand whether we\'re talking about a sequencing issue or a fundamental capacity one."',
['Say, "I understand it\'s a busy time — but this can\'t wait."','Concede: "You\'re probably right — maybe we should wait."','Say, "I think the team can manage — we\'ve done it before."'],
'Asking specifically which commitments are in conflict separates a legitimate capacity concern from a general resistance to change — and gives you information to work with.',
'"This can\'t wait" overrides a real concern with urgency. Conceding without understanding the specific conflict may be unnecessary. "The team can manage — we\'ve done it before" dismisses the concern without engaging with it.'),
mcq(4,'You learn that one specific commitment conflicts directly with your launch month. You have flexibility on the date. How do you respond?',
'"If we move the launch from June to September, we avoid that conflict entirely. Does that solve the capacity concern?"',
['Insist on June: "The business case is strongest in June — we can\'t move it."','Offer to remove the conflicting resource from the other commitment instead.','Say, "Let me think about the timing and come back to you."'],
'A specific, immediate concession that directly resolves the named conflict — moving the date — is both fair and strategically smart. It turns an objection into an agreement.',
'Insisting on June when you have flexibility is stubbornness that costs buy-in for no reason. Removing the resource from the other commitment could create a new problem. Saying you will think about it and come back is a delay when you already have a solution.'),
mcq(5,'The last objection: "I\'m not sure leadership will support this." How do you address it?',
'"I\'ve already spoken with the director and she has indicated she is supportive in principle. I\'d like to have a joint update with her and the team before we go further — would that address the concern?"',
['Say, "Leadership will come around once they see the results."','Use the objection to argue that leadership support is not what matters here.','Say, "I\'m not sure either — but I think we should try anyway."'],
'Citing specific leadership support already obtained, and offering a joint update to make that visible, converts the objection into a next step.',
'"Leadership will come around" is an assertion you cannot substantiate. Arguing leadership support does not matter when someone has just raised it as a concern dismisses the concern. "I\'m not sure either but let\'s try" confirms the concern rather than addressing it.')],
'response':resp('You treated every objection as a serious question, connected each one to a specific response in your proposal, found a real flexibility on timing when a capacity conflict was identified, and converted the final concern about leadership support into a next step. Now type or say aloud the objection-handling matrix you would build before your next presentation.',
'Brilliant! Objections handled well are the best possible outcome — they tell you exactly what your audience needs to believe before they can say yes. You welcomed every one of them today and used each as a building block. That is persuasion mastery.')})

levels.append({'id':79,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Leading with Curiosity',
'scenario':'You are in a conversation with a senior colleague who holds very different views on your department\'s direction. You disagree with her direction but you want to influence her thinking — not by arguing for your position but by asking questions that invite her to examine her own assumptions. You are going to lead with curiosity.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You want to understand her position before you try to influence it. How do you open?',
'"I\'d really like to understand your thinking on this direction. Can you walk me through what problem you\'re trying to solve — what does success look like for you in twelve months?"',
['Say, "I have a different view and I\'d like to share it."','Ask leading questions designed to expose flaws in her reasoning.','Stay quiet and let her talk, then wait for an opening to make your point.'],
'Understanding the problem she is trying to solve and what success looks like to her is the foundational insight you need before you can genuinely influence her direction.',
'"I have a different view and I\'d like to share it" opens with your position rather than her thinking. Leading questions designed to expose flaws is a debate tactic, not curiosity. Waiting for an opening to make your point means you have not really been listening.'),
mcq(2,'She explains her thinking and you notice that her success criteria depends on a market assumption that you think is questionable. How do you respond?',
'"That\'s helpful context. The assumption that the market will grow at X% — where does that come from? I want to understand how central that is to the overall direction."',
['Say, "That assumption seems risky — I\'m not sure the market growth will be there."','Cite your own data that contradicts her assumption immediately.','Say nothing and note it for later — you don\'t want to seem critical too early.'],
'A curious, non-threatening question about the source and centrality of the assumption invites her to examine it without feeling attacked.',
'Immediately characterising the assumption as risky puts her on the defensive. Citing contradictory data immediately is a debate move. Noting it for later misses the opportunity to explore it when it arises naturally.'),
mcq(3,'She says, "The growth assumption is from our market research last year." You know there is more recent data that tells a different story. How do you introduce it?',
'"I\'ve seen some more recent data that tells a slightly different story — would it be useful to look at it together? I\'m genuinely curious whether it changes the picture or whether I\'m missing something."',
['Say, "Our most recent data contradicts that — I think you should update the assumption."','Let her continue without introducing the more recent data, to avoid conflict.','Ask a question designed to make her realise her data is old without telling her.'],
'Offering the data as something to explore together, with genuine openness about whether it changes things, is curiosity in action. It respects her intelligence and keeps the door open.',
'"Contradicts that — you should update" is direct but slightly combative and removes the collaborative quality of the conversation. Staying silent means a false assumption may drive a major direction. Tricking her into realising her data is old is a manipulation rather than curiosity.'),
mcq(4,'She looks at the more recent data and says, "Hmm — this is more conservative than ours. I hadn\'t seen this." How do you respond?',
'"I found it last week — I wasn\'t sure if it had come across. What does this change for your success criteria, if anything?"',
['Say, "I thought you might not have seen it — this changes things significantly."','Immediately outline your alternative direction now that she seems open.','Let her process it in silence without asking any questions.'],
'Naming when you found it — without suggesting she was negligent — and immediately returning the analysis to her with a curious question is generous and keeps the ownership with her.',
'"I thought you might not have seen it" sounds slightly superior. Immediately outlining your alternative direction hijacks the moment of her genuine reconsideration. Letting her process in silence misses the opportunity to guide where her reconsideration goes.'),
mcq(5,'She says, "I think we need to revisit the growth assumption in our model — this matters." How do you respond?',
'"I\'m glad we looked at it together. What would be most useful — for me to send you the full dataset, or should we schedule time to work through the model update as a group?"',
['Say, "Yes — I think that\'s the right call. And while we\'re at it, let me share my alternative direction."','Say, "I\'ve already updated the model with the new data — I can send you the revised version."','Just say, "Great — let me know if I can help."'],
'Confirming you are glad you looked at it together, and offering two specific next steps that keep her in the driver\'s seat, is the ideal close to a curiosity-led influence conversation.',
'"Let me share my alternative direction" uses her moment of openness to convert it into an argument for your position — which undermines the curiosity that got you there. Saying you have already updated the model removes her agency from the reconsideration. "Let me know if I can help" is too passive.')],
'response':resp('You opened by understanding the problem she was trying to solve, asked a non-threatening question about a key assumption, introduced newer data as something to explore together, let her reach her own reconsideration, and offered specific next steps that kept her in control. Now type or say aloud the message you would send her after the conversation to confirm the agreed next step.',
'Remarkable! Leading with curiosity is one of the most sophisticated and powerful influence strategies there is. You changed her direction by helping her think more clearly — not by arguing for your own. That is leadership through understanding.')})

levels.append({'id':80,'journey_id':8,'journey_title':'Influence & Persuasion','title':'Long-Term Influence',
'scenario':'You want to build genuine long-term influence in your professional community — not through a single persuasive act but through the consistent, generous, credible presence over time that makes people seek your perspective and trust your judgment. You are thinking about influence as a practice, not an event.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You have identified that you want to be a go-to voice on a specific topic in your field. What is the most important first step?',
'Develop a distinctive, evidence-based point of view on the topic — something specific and defensible that you can articulate in one clear paragraph.',
['Build a large social media following first to create visibility.','Position yourself as a generalist who can speak to many topics across your field.','Start attending as many industry events as possible to meet people.'],
'A specific, defensible, evidence-based point of view is the substance that all long-term influence is built on. Without it, visibility is hollow.',
'A large following without a distinctive view produces noise, not influence. Being a generalist reduces the depth of your expertise signal. Attending events is valuable but only if you have something specific and interesting to say.'),
mcq(2,'A junior colleague asks for your perspective on a topic you know well. How do you respond in a way that builds long-term influence?',
'Give a considered, specific, honest response — including the parts that are counterintuitive or that go against conventional wisdom in your field.',
['Give a brief, safe, mainstream answer to avoid controversy.','Refer them to an article or resource rather than giving your own view.','Tell them you will think about it and follow up, rather than answering off the top of your head.'],
'Counterintuitive, honest perspectives are what people remember and come back for. Safe mainstream answers can be found anywhere.',
'A safe mainstream answer builds no distinctiveness. Referring to a resource rather than giving your own view is helpful but not influence-building. "I\'ll think about it and follow up" is fine occasionally but if done habitually signals you are reluctant to commit to a position.'),
mcq(3,'You share an opinion publicly — in a presentation or article — and someone challenges it with a strong counterpoint. How do you respond?',
'Engage the counterpoint specifically and directly: acknowledge what is strong about it, hold what you still believe, and explain the reasoning for the difference.',
['Ignore the challenge to avoid conflict.','Capitulate immediately: "You raise a good point — I think you\'re probably right."','Dismiss the challenge: "I\'m confident in my position — I don\'t think the counterpoint holds."'],
'Engaging a counterpoint specifically — acknowledging its strength while explaining where you still differ — is what builds intellectual credibility. It signals you can hold a view and think at the same time.',
'Ignoring a direct challenge damages your credibility as a thinker. Immediate capitulation suggests your position was not firmly held. Dismissing the counterpoint as not holding without explanation is just stubbornness, not confidence.'),
mcq(4,'You make a public mistake on a topic — you stated something that turns out to be wrong. How do you handle it?',
'Publicly correct yourself: "I was wrong about X — here is what the correct position is and why I missed it. Lesson learned."',
['Stay quiet and hope no one noticed.','Defend the original position even though you now know it was wrong.','Attribute the error to outdated information you were given by others.'],
'Public self-correction — naming specifically what was wrong and the lesson — is the single best credibility-building act you can make when you have been wrong. It is far more trust-building than being right.',
'Staying quiet hopes for the best in a way that rarely holds. Defending a position you know to be wrong is a serious integrity failure. Attributing the error to others undermines your personal intellectual ownership.'),
mcq(5,'After several years of consistent, specific, honest engagement on your topic, a senior leader in your field says, "I always look forward to your perspective on this — you\'re one of the people I read first." How do you respond?',
'"Thank you — that genuinely means a lot to me. I\'ve tried to be honest and specific rather than popular and I\'m glad that\'s what comes across."',
['Immediately mention your latest article or piece of work.','Deflect: "I\'m just sharing what I find — nothing special really."','Say, "That\'s very kind — I find your work equally valuable!" and leave it at that.'],
'Receiving the specific compliment warmly, naming the deliberate quality you have aimed for — honest and specific rather than popular — and affirming that it lands as intended is confident and complete.',
'Immediately promoting your latest work uses a generous moment for self-promotion. Deflecting as "nothing special" undercuts a year of deliberate effort. A generic "I find your work equally valuable" is social politeness but not a genuine response to a specific compliment.')],
'response':resp('You developed a specific point of view, gave honest counterintuitive perspectives when asked, engaged a challenge with intellectual courage, publicly corrected a mistake with full ownership, and received a long-term influence compliment with genuine, honest acknowledgment. Now type or say aloud the one distinctive, evidence-based position on your topic that you would want your professional community to associate with your name.',
'Outstanding! Long-term influence is not built in a single persuasive moment — it is built in thousands of small choices about what to say, how honestly to say it, and whether to hold your position when challenged. You showed what all of those choices look like. That is influence that lasts.')})
